1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,567
Suppose you send a friend a private message:
meet at seven.

2
00:00:04,567 --> 00:00:09,133
On its journey, someone else might see the
data. If the

3
00:00:09,133 --> 00:00:13,700
message is readable, that person can read it
too. So we

4
00:00:13,700 --> 00:00:18,267
want to change it into something they cannot
understand, then let

5
00:00:18,267 --> 00:00:22,833
our friend change it back. That process is
encryption. A simple

6
00:00:22,833 --> 00:00:27,400
approach uses a shared secret. But here is a
puzzle: how

7
00:00:27,400 --> 00:00:31,967
do we send privately to someone before we have
shared that

8
00:00:31,967 --> 00:00:36,533
secret?

9
00:00:36,533 --> 00:00:41,100
Picture your friend handing out open padlocks
while keeping the opening

10
00:00:41,100 --> 00:00:45,667
key. Anyone can put a message in a box and
snap

11
00:00:45,667 --> 00:00:50,233
a lock shut. Only your friend can open it. RSA
uses

12
00:00:50,233 --> 00:00:54,800
a mathematical version of two different jobs:
a public rule for

13
00:00:54,800 --> 00:00:59,367
encrypting and a private rule for recovering
the message. Computers represent

14
00:00:59,367 --> 00:01:03,933
messages as numbers, so our tiny demonstration
will protect the number

15
00:01:03,933 --> 00:01:08,500
seven. The padlock is an analogy; the real
work will be

16
00:01:08,500 --> 00:01:13,067
arithmetic.

17
00:01:13,067 --> 00:01:17,971
Before any keys, we need one easy operation: a
remainder. Put

18
00:01:17,971 --> 00:01:22,876
eleven counters into groups of five. Two full
groups use ten

19
00:01:22,876 --> 00:01:27,781
counters, leaving one. Eleven divided by five
has remainder one. The

20
00:01:27,781 --> 00:01:32,686
short mathematical name is modulo: eleven mod
five equals one. We

21
00:01:32,686 --> 00:01:37,590
will later use groups of fifty-five, but the
idea is identical.

22
00:01:37,590 --> 00:01:42,495
Keep what is left after removing complete
groups. No mysterious new

23
00:01:42,495 --> 00:01:47,400
kind of number, just the leftover amount.

24
00:01:47,400 --> 00:01:52,079
Choose five and eleven. Both are prime
numbers: whole numbers greater

25
00:01:52,079 --> 00:01:56,758
than one that divide evenly only by one and
themselves. Primes

26
00:01:56,758 --> 00:02:01,438
act like building blocks for multiplication.
Multiply our two choices and

27
00:02:01,438 --> 00:02:06,117
we get fifty-five. That becomes the number we
will use when

28
00:02:06,117 --> 00:02:10,796
keeping remainders. We call it n, simply a
short label. Our

29
00:02:10,796 --> 00:02:15,475
public instructions will include fifty-five.
The two factors, five and eleven,

30
00:02:15,475 --> 00:02:20,154
help us build the private instructions. These
tiny numbers are for

31
00:02:20,154 --> 00:02:24,833
seeing the idea.

32
00:02:24,833 --> 00:02:29,567
Why did we keep those two factors? They help
describe a

33
00:02:29,567 --> 00:02:34,300
repeating pattern in the remainder arithmetic.
Look at the numbers from

34
00:02:34,300 --> 00:02:39,033
zero through fifty-four. Cross out multiples
of five and multiples of

35
00:02:39,033 --> 00:02:43,767
eleven. Forty positions remain. None of those
shares a factor with

36
00:02:43,767 --> 00:02:48,500
fifty-five except one. For these primes, we
can count them as

37
00:02:48,500 --> 00:02:53,233
four times ten: forty. Keep that number
nearby. We will use

38
00:02:53,233 --> 00:02:57,967
its repeating structure to choose two
operations that work together, rather

39
00:02:57,967 --> 00:03:02,700
than guessing a secret.

40
00:03:02,700 --> 00:03:07,795
Let our public instruction be: multiply three
copies of the message

41
00:03:07,795 --> 00:03:12,890
number together, then keep the remainder after
dividing by fifty-five. Three

42
00:03:12,890 --> 00:03:17,986
copies multiplied together is called the third
power, or cubing. We

43
00:03:17,986 --> 00:03:23,081
label that power e, and choose e equal to
three. The

44
00:03:23,081 --> 00:03:28,176
public key is just the pair fifty-five and
three. Why this

45
00:03:28,176 --> 00:03:33,271
choice? Three and our count of forty share no
factor above

46
00:03:33,271 --> 00:03:38,367
one, which lets us find a matching private
power next.

47
00:03:38,367 --> 00:03:43,524
Now find a number which, when multiplied by
three, leaves one

48
00:03:43,524 --> 00:03:48,681
after removing complete groups of forty.
Twenty-seven works. Three times twenty-seven

49
00:03:48,681 --> 00:03:53,838
is eighty-one, and eighty-one is forty plus
forty plus one. We

50
00:03:53,838 --> 00:03:58,995
label the matching private power d, so d is
twenty-seven. Notice

51
00:03:58,995 --> 00:04:04,152
the important part: this number was chosen to
fit the public

52
00:04:04,152 --> 00:04:09,310
operation. It is not an unrelated password.
The relationship between three,

53
00:04:09,310 --> 00:04:14,467
twenty-seven, and forty is what will let the
two operations cooperate.

54
00:04:14,467 --> 00:04:19,562
Time to send our tiny message, seven. Follow
the public rule:

55
00:04:19,562 --> 00:04:24,657
seven times seven times seven is three hundred
and forty-three. Remove

56
00:04:24,657 --> 00:04:29,752
complete groups of fifty-five. Six groups
account for three hundred and

57
00:04:29,752 --> 00:04:34,848
thirty, leaving thirteen. That leftover number
is the encrypted result, also

58
00:04:34,848 --> 00:04:39,943
called ciphertext. Seven has become thirteen.
Anyone with the public pair

59
00:04:39,943 --> 00:04:45,038
fifty-five and three can perform this forward
calculation. We have now

60
00:04:45,038 --> 00:04:50,133
watched encryption happen, using only
multiplication and keeping a remainder.

61
00:04:50,133 --> 00:04:55,229
The private rule asks us to use a much bigger
power.

62
00:04:55,229 --> 00:05:00,324
Must we write an enormous number first?
Happily, no. We can

63
00:05:00,324 --> 00:05:05,419
keep remainders while calculating. Start with
thirteen. Square it, meaning multiply

64
00:05:05,419 --> 00:05:10,514
it by itself: one hundred and sixty-nine
leaves four after division

65
00:05:10,514 --> 00:05:15,610
by fifty-five. Square that four to get
sixteen. Square sixteen and

66
00:05:15,610 --> 00:05:20,705
keep its remainder, thirty-six. Each step
keeps the same useful remainder

67
00:05:20,705 --> 00:05:25,800
information while preventing the working
numbers from growing without limit.

68
00:05:25,800 --> 00:05:30,425
Now use the private power, twenty-seven. We
can split it into

69
00:05:30,425 --> 00:05:35,050
sixteen plus eight plus two plus one. The
boxes show the

70
00:05:35,050 --> 00:05:39,675
remainders of thirteen raised to those powers.
Multiply those values together,

71
00:05:39,675 --> 00:05:44,300
keeping the remainder after each step. Watch
the result settle on

72
00:05:44,300 --> 00:05:48,925
seven. We recovered the original message.
Written compactly, thirteen to the

73
00:05:48,925 --> 00:05:53,550
power twenty-seven, mod fifty-five, equals
seven. The arithmetic looks longer than

74
00:05:53,550 --> 00:05:58,175
encryption, but it uses the same two actions:
multiply and keep

75
00:05:58,175 --> 00:06:02,800
a remainder.

76
00:06:02,800 --> 00:06:07,479
The interesting question is why seven returns.
For numbers such as

77
00:06:07,479 --> 00:06:12,158
seven, which share no factor with fifty-five
except one, these powers

78
00:06:12,158 --> 00:06:16,838
have a repeating structure linked to our count
of forty. Encrypting

79
00:06:16,838 --> 00:06:21,517
with power three and decrypting with power
twenty-seven combines into power

80
00:06:21,517 --> 00:06:26,196
eighty-one. That is one plus two times forty.
The matching construction

81
00:06:26,196 --> 00:06:30,875
brings us back to the original value. The
circle illustrates repetition;

82
00:06:30,875 --> 00:06:35,554
it is not a claim that every message takes
exactly forty

83
00:06:35,554 --> 00:06:40,233
steps to repeat.

84
00:06:40,233 --> 00:06:45,329
What if we replace twenty-seven with a guess?
Use five instead.

85
00:06:45,329 --> 00:06:50,424
The same encrypted number thirteen now
produces forty-three, not seven. The

86
00:06:50,424 --> 00:06:55,519
right side uses twenty-seven and recovers
seven. So the private rule

87
00:06:55,519 --> 00:07:00,614
is not simply any large number or any secret
number. It

88
00:07:00,614 --> 00:07:05,710
must fit the public construction. This small
experiment does not prove

89
00:07:05,710 --> 00:07:10,805
security. It shows what correctness requires:
the intended pair must undo

90
00:07:10,805 --> 00:07:15,900
the message transformation, while an arbitrary
guess generally does not.

91
00:07:15,900 --> 00:07:20,467
Our public key contains fifty-five, but not
its two prime factors.

92
00:07:20,467 --> 00:07:25,033
If someone discovers five and eleven, they can
repeat our private-key

93
00:07:25,033 --> 00:07:29,600
construction: calculate forty, then find the
matching power twenty-seven. For fifty-five

94
00:07:29,600 --> 00:07:34,167
that is easy, which is why this example
protects nothing. Real

95
00:07:34,167 --> 00:07:38,733
RSA uses far larger carefully generated
primes. Multiplying them is practical,

96
00:07:38,733 --> 00:07:43,300
while recovering their factors from the
product is intended to be

97
00:07:43,300 --> 00:07:47,867
difficult. The security depends on such
mathematical assumptions and on correct

98
00:07:47,867 --> 00:07:52,433
implementation.

99
00:07:52,433 --> 00:07:57,167
There is another reason not to use our little
calculation directly

100
00:07:57,167 --> 00:08:01,900
as real encryption. Encrypt seven twice with
this bare rule and

101
00:08:01,900 --> 00:08:06,633
thirteen appears twice. That visible
repetition can leak information. Practical
encryption

102
00:08:06,633 --> 00:08:11,367
needs a complete, carefully designed encoding
scheme that adds the right

103
00:08:11,367 --> 00:08:16,100
structure and randomness before the RSA
operation. We are looking inside

104
00:08:16,100 --> 00:08:20,833
one mathematical mechanism, not building a
safe messaging app. The small

105
00:08:20,833 --> 00:08:25,567
numbers let us understand the moving parts
without pretending that understanding

106
00:08:25,567 --> 00:08:30,300
alone makes them secure.

107
00:08:30,300 --> 00:08:34,867
Step back from the arithmetic. With a shared-
secret method, the people

108
00:08:34,867 --> 00:08:39,433
communicating must have a suitable secret in
common. With this public-key

109
00:08:39,433 --> 00:08:44,000
arrangement, the receiving person can publish
the encrypting instructions and keep

110
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:48,567
the recovery instructions private. The private
key is related to the

111
00:08:48,567 --> 00:08:53,133
public key, but should be impractical to
derive from it. That

112
00:08:53,133 --> 00:08:57,700
last condition matters. Publishing an
operation is useful only if seeing

113
00:08:57,700 --> 00:09:02,267
how to perform it does not make recovering the
private operation

114
00:09:02,267 --> 00:09:06,833
easy.

115
00:09:06,833 --> 00:09:11,567
We wanted to send a private message without
first handing the

116
00:09:11,567 --> 00:09:16,300
sender our opening secret. The lock picture
suggested separate public and

117
00:09:16,300 --> 00:09:21,033
private jobs. Our tiny RSA example made those
jobs concrete: seven

118
00:09:21,033 --> 00:09:25,767
became thirteen using the public pair fifty-
five and three; thirteen returned

119
00:09:25,767 --> 00:09:30,500
to seven using the private power twenty-seven.
Remainders kept the arithmetic

120
00:09:30,500 --> 00:09:35,233
inside a limited set of values. The carefully
chosen relationship made

121
00:09:35,233 --> 00:09:39,967
recovery work. That is the idea beneath RSA:
shared public instructions,

122
00:09:39,967 --> 00:09:44,700
paired with private recovery.
